# Third-party binaries — fetch them, don't commit them.
#
# Not a licensing prohibition. tun2proxy is MIT, and Wintun's signed DLL is
# redistributable when it ships beside software that uses it via the Permitted
# API — which is exactly what upstream tun2proxy does. Only adb is unclear:
# Google's signed platform-tools build falls under the Android SDK agreement,
# which forbids redistribution (3.4) yet exempts open-source components (3.5),
# and adb itself is Apache 2.0 in AOSP.
#
# The reasons are size and publication. These five were 39 MB of a 96 MB repo,
# and this repository is served wholesale as a GitHub Pages site, so whatever
# lands here is downloadable from the web. output/README.md says where to get
# them, with the archive paths verified.
#
# Match by name, not by folder: the older rule below covered only pc/, so
# output/wintun.dll slipped past it.
wintun.dll
tun2proxy
tun2proxy.exe
adb
adb.exe
